{"version_id": 55, "article_id": 6, "level_code": "en_c1", "lang": "en", "level": 5, "level_label": "C1 高级", "title": "Fairer rules needed to sustain global science collaboration amid conflict", "title_zh": "冲突之下，全球科研合作需要更公平的规则", "topic": "science", "source": "nature", "source_url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03176-y", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49705390741_8c22b1fd8a_b.jpg", "credit": "USDAgov · pdm 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 221, "reading_minutes": 1.1, "lead": "Scientific collaboration has traditionally been seen as a rare sphere that rises above geopolitics. Yet as international tensions spill into research, a troubling side effect is taking hold: where a scientist works is increasingly read as a signal of political allegiance.", "lead_zh": "科研合作一向被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域。然而，随着国际紧张局势蔓延到科研界，一个令人担忧的副作用正在显现：科学家的工作单位越来越被当作政治立场的标志。", "preview": "Scientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. However, as global tensions i", "preview_zh": "长期以来，科研合作一直被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。然而，随着全球紧张局势日益左右国际研究，一个意想不到的后果正在浮现：机构隶属关系正变成政治身份的替身。这种转变可能使研究人员受到惩罚，原因不是他们的工作，而是他们恰好所在的国家。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 45, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/audio/6/en_c1.mp3", "duration": 99.925, "size_bytes": 603216, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 248, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/data/versions/55.json", "paragraphs": ["Scientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity. This shift risks penalising researchers not for their work, but for the country in which they happen to be based.", "The authors, Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh of Shanghai University and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi of Zhejiang Normal University, argue that maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules. Writing in Nature, they suggest that existing frameworks for research partnerships have not kept pace with the realities of a more fractured world, leaving scientists exposed to suspicion and exclusion.", "The correspondence appears against a backdrop of rising geopolitical friction and widening academic boycotts. Related coverage notes that such tensions are prompting China to rethink its research partnerships, while other commentators warn that global geopolitics should not stall science. The authors' intervention adds to a growing debate about how to keep scientific exchange open without ignoring legitimate security concerns.", "Details of the proposed rules are not disclosed in the available text, but the core message is clear: without fairer and more transparent arrangements, the universalist ideals that have long animated international science may erode. The piece is published in Nature, volume 658, page 572 (2026)."], "paragraphs_zh": ["长期以来，科研合作一直被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。然而，随着全球紧张局势日益左右国际研究，一个意想不到的后果正在浮现：机构隶属关系正变成政治身份的替身。这种转变可能使研究人员受到惩罚，原因不是他们的工作，而是他们恰好所在的国家。", "文章作者、上海大学的 Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh 和浙江师范大学的 Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi 认为，在冲突期间维持全球科学合作需要更公平的规则。他们在《自然》杂志上撰文指出，现有的研究合作框架未能跟上更加分裂的世界的现实，使科学家容易遭受怀疑和排斥。", "这篇通讯文章发表之际，地缘政治摩擦加剧，学术抵制运动不断扩大。相关报道指出，此类紧张局势正促使中国重新考虑其研究伙伴关系，而其他评论者则警告，全球地缘政治不应阻碍科学发展。作者的介入为一场日益升温的辩论增添了声音：如何在不让合理安全关切被忽视的前提下，保持科学交流的开放。", "现有文本未披露拟议规则的具体细节，但核心信息十分明确：如果没有更公平、更透明的安排，长期激励国际科学界的普世理想可能会受到侵蚀。该文发表于《自然》第 658 卷，第 572 页（2026 年）。"], "body": "Scientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity. This shift risks penalising researchers not for their work, but for the country in which they happen to be based.\n\nThe authors, Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh of Shanghai University and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi of Zhejiang Normal University, argue that maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules. Writing in Nature, they suggest that existing frameworks for research partnerships have not kept pace with the realities of a more fractured world, leaving scientists exposed to suspicion and exclusion.\n\nThe correspondence appears against a backdrop of rising geopolitical friction and widening academic boycotts. Related coverage notes that such tensions are prompting China to rethink its research partnerships, while other commentators warn that global geopolitics should not stall science. The authors' intervention adds to a growing debate about how to keep scientific exchange open without ignoring legitimate security concerns.\n\nDetails of the proposed rules are not disclosed in the available text, but the core message is clear: without fairer and more transparent arrangements, the universalist ideals that have long animated international science may erode. The piece is published in Nature, volume 658, page 572 (2026).", "body_zh": "长期以来，科研合作一直被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。然而，随着全球紧张局势日益左右国际研究，一个意想不到的后果正在浮现：机构隶属关系正变成政治身份的替身。这种转变可能使研究人员受到惩罚，原因不是他们的工作，而是他们恰好所在的国家。\n\n文章作者、上海大学的 Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh 和浙江师范大学的 Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi 认为，在冲突期间维持全球科学合作需要更公平的规则。他们在《自然》杂志上撰文指出，现有的研究合作框架未能跟上更加分裂的世界的现实，使科学家容易遭受怀疑和排斥。\n\n这篇通讯文章发表之际，地缘政治摩擦加剧，学术抵制运动不断扩大。相关报道指出，此类紧张局势正促使中国重新考虑其研究伙伴关系，而其他评论者则警告，全球地缘政治不应阻碍科学发展。作者的介入为一场日益升温的辩论增添了声音：如何在不让合理安全关切被忽视的前提下，保持科学交流的开放。\n\n现有文本未披露拟议规则的具体细节，但核心信息十分明确：如果没有更公平、更透明的安排，长期激励国际科学界的普世理想可能会受到侵蚀。该文发表于《自然》第 658 卷，第 572 页（2026 年）。", "text": "Scientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity. This shift risks penalising researchers not for their work, but for the country in which they happen to be based. The authors, Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh of Shanghai University and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi of Zhejiang Normal University, argue that maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules. Writing in Nature, they suggest that existing frameworks for research partnerships have not kept pace with the realities of a more fractured world, leaving scientists exposed to suspicion and exclusion. The correspondence appears against a backdrop of rising geopolitical friction and widening academic boycotts. Related coverage notes that such tensions are prompting China to rethink its research partnerships, while other commentators warn that global geopolitics should not stall science. The authors' intervention adds to a growing debate about how to keep scientific exchange open without ignoring legitimate security concerns. Details of the proposed rules are not disclosed in the available text, but the core message is clear: without fairer and more transparent arrangements, the universalist ideals that have long animated international science may erode. The piece is published in Nature, volume 658, page 572 (2026).", "vocab": [{"word": "transcend", "pos": "verb", "zh": "超越，超脱", "note": "常与 geopolitics、boundaries 等搭配，表示不受其限制"}, {"word": "geopolitics", "pos": "noun", "zh": "地缘政治", "note": "国际关系中的核心术语，指地理与政治权力的互动"}, {"word": "unintended consequence", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "意想不到的后果", "note": "政策或行动带来的非预期结果，常用于学术与新闻写作"}, {"word": "institutional affiliation", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "机构隶属关系", "note": "指研究者所属的大学或研究机构"}, {"word": "proxy", "pos": "noun", "zh": "替代指标，替身", "note": "此处指用机构归属来代表政治身份"}, {"word": "penalise", "pos": "verb", "zh": "惩罚，处罚", "note": "英式拼写，美式为 penalize"}, {"word": "fractured", "pos": "adj", "zh": "分裂的，破碎的", "note": "形容世界或社会因矛盾而四分五裂"}, {"word": "keep pace with", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "跟上……的步伐", "note": "表示与某事物同步发展，不落后"}, {"word": "backdrop", "pos": "noun", "zh": "背景", "note": "常与 against the backdrop of 搭配，引出大环境"}, {"word": "friction", "pos": "noun", "zh": "摩擦，冲突", "note": "可指人际或国际关系中的紧张状态"}, {"word": "boycott", "pos": "noun", "zh": "抵制", "note": "此处指学术抵制，即拒绝与某些机构合作"}, {"word": "intervention", "pos": "noun", "zh": "介入，发言", "note": "在辩论或公共讨论中发表意见的行为"}, {"word": "erode", "pos": "verb", "zh": "侵蚀，削弱", "note": "常用于抽象事物，如信任、理想、权利等逐渐受损"}, {"word": "universalist", "pos": "adj", "zh": "普世主义的", "note": "指认为某些价值或原则适用于全人类的观念"}], "title_original": "Maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "summary_original": "Nature, Published online: 06 October 2026;\ndoi:10.1038/d41586-026-03176-y\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "body_original": "CORRESPONDENCE\n06 October 2026\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules\nBy\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\n0\n&\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\n1\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\nShanghai University, Shanghai, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\nZhejiang Normal University, Jinhua, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEmail\nBluesky\nFacebook\nLinkedIn\nReddit\nWhatsapp\nX\nSave article\nView saved research\nScientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity.\nAccess options\nAccess through your institution\nAccess Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\nGet Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription\n$32.99\n/ 30 days\ncancel any tim